=== DIAGNOSIS ===
is_anomaly: true
faulty_devices: router6
diagnosis_text:
**Affected component:** iptables FORWARD chain on router6, specifically rules applied to traffic ingressing on `eth2` (the WAN uplink, 10.50.1.2/24, connecting to router3 at 10.50.1.1).

**Mechanism:** Router6 is the sole gateway for the 172.18.51.0/24 subnet (its `eth1` is 172.18.51.254, which is the configured default gateway for pc15–pc18). Three DROP rules in its FORWARD chain block **all** TCP, UDP, and ICMP traffic that enters the router on `eth2`. The only permitted traffic through eth2 is HTTP (TCP/80) from 172.16.10.0/24 to 172.18.51.1. Because all cross-subnet return traffic and all inbound traffic from other sites arrives at router6 via eth2 (coming from router3), it is silently dropped before being forwarded to the 172.18.51.x hosts. Outbound traffic from the 172.18.51.x hosts successfully leaves (enters on eth1, not blocked), but every reply is discarded at router6's FORWARD chain. Live packet counters confirm 26 ICMP packets dropped.

**Localization:** router6, iptables FORWARD chain, `--in-interface eth2`. All three DROP rules (TCP proto 6, UDP proto 17, ICMP proto 0) are on this single device and interface.

**Root cause:** Misconfigured iptables FORWARD rules on router6 drop all returning and inbound transit traffic on the WAN-facing interface `eth2`, isolating the entire 172.18.51.0/24 subnet from the rest of the network.

**Fix:** Remove (or replace) the three blanket DROP rules from the FORWARD chain on router6. The correct policy is to allow established/related sessions (`-m state --state ESTABLISHED,RELATED -j ACCEPT`) and any needed inbound services, then drop the rest if a restrictive policy is required.
=== END ===